{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-40194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(21[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-40194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-140120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-140120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{7\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{7\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{7\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2}{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-140120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-140120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-140120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-140120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81472" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81472" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 2+(-12)\\cdot (-2)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 2+(-12)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-12)\\cdot (-2)}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81472" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-81472" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81472" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81472" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{20\\cdot 4+(-20)\\cdot 5+0\\cdot 0}{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=-\\frac{20}{41}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{20\\cdot 5+(-20)\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 0}{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{180}{41}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{41}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{180}{41}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{41}(-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The projection is the vector $x$ itself. This means that $x$ can be written as a linear combination of ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ In other words, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-40616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-39992" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set \n${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-39992" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-39992" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-39992" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-39992" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-39992" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=-1[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81591" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-81591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81591" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-40192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 31\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-4)+31\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+31\\cdot (-2)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}w=-5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-81593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-13 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-2)+(-16)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-3)+(-16)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-3)}{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-81585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-81585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-40666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-5)\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+(-5)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{3}=(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ coincides with the projection, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is zero $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-82915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The fact that we got the zero-vector as the projection means that $x$ is orthogonal to the subspace $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 4}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-\\frac{2}{3})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{2}{3})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}([\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x$ coincides with the projection, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is zero $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-40610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2-\\frac{9}{4})}^{2}+{(1-(-\\frac{3}{4}))}^{2}+{(-3-(-\\frac{7}{4}))}^{2}+{(1-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{49}{16}+\\frac{25}{16}+\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{76}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82914" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-82914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{19}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{38}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82914" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{9}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-3)^{2}+(0-0)^{2}+(-3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-40605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "27"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "9"] ["math" "9\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-140451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-140451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{6\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{6\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 3+(-4)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{\\sqrt{53}\\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{29}{53}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\sqrt{\\frac{29}{53}})≈{42.29}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-140451" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-140451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-140451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{41.39}^{∘}"] ["math" "{42.29}^{∘}"] ["math" "{44.92}^{∘}"] ["math" "{45.23}^{∘}"] ["math" "{48.32}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-140451" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{42.29}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{5\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{36}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{6\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-82950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 2+7\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+7\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{a}_{3}=(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{\\sqrt{63}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})≈{28.13}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-82978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{28.13}^{∘}"] ["math" "{29.91}^{∘}"] ["math" "{36.32}^{∘}"] ["math" "{48.59}^{∘}"] ["math" "{7.18}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82978" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{28.13}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{44}\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2\\sqrt{11}\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11})≈{25.24}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-82948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{11.12}^{∘}"] ["math" "{12.11}^{∘}"] ["math" "{18.25}^{∘}"] ["math" "{25.24}^{∘}"] ["math" "{29.21}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-82948" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{25.24}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-1)}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,9\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 11+(-1)\\cdot (-7)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{11}^{2}+(-7)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{171}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{57}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{57}}{9})≈{32.98}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3944de6a3727f3f405b7dd162373a24dc2a6fe43affdd3ae40a780ce8bceb465"} {"question" "q-40223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{23.98}^{∘}"] ["math" "{29.38}^{∘}"] ["math" "{32.98}^{∘}"] ["math" "{38.29}^{∘}"] ["math" "{83.29}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "q-40223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{32.98}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b640c8072dd44431d7f23426bae778cd55673c656c37bab4fe9d2300e568ac2"} {"question" "e-5573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-5573.json" "file_sha256" "ecb104155aeae61a2ab41c4c0d3e119b278e0e484a85827f7a85642a79a34435"} {"question" "e-5573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=1[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-5573.json" "file_sha256" "ecb104155aeae61a2ab41c4c0d3e119b278e0e484a85827f7a85642a79a34435"} {"question" "e-7173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w},$ where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane given that $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-7173.json" "file_sha256" "a0e6a36f3154b2a41f97bf583ff4c86808b27498e9f46a8823a9137f019d8975"} {"question" "e-7173" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by   \n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\,\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{13\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}v=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{13\\cdot 5+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{{5}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-7173.json" "file_sha256" "a0e6a36f3154b2a41f97bf583ff4c86808b27498e9f46a8823a9137f019d8975"} {"question" "e-5574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-5574.json" "file_sha256" "354577d0d954af00ccf824bd3ff9351a93771a41b5d4a8130ab20917bfa49a7a"} {"question" "e-5574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3-2)}^{2}+{(2-2)}^{2}+{(1-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-5574.json" "file_sha256" "354577d0d954af00ccf824bd3ff9351a93771a41b5d4a8130ab20917bfa49a7a"} {"question" "e-7309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf v,\\mathbf w\\}$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-7309.json" "file_sha256" "1e3776107486f2968a71731a723aa2760c220042c1d8b831b95deb7b7608e267"} {"question" "e-7309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \n= \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}w=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}.$ So, computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{10})}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{20}\\,\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{10\\,\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left( \\dfrac{{\\sqrt2}}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040677/example-7309.json" "file_sha256" "1e3776107486f2968a71731a723aa2760c220042c1d8b831b95deb7b7608e267"}] "usage" {"q-40194" [] "q-40666" [] "e-5573" [] "e-5574" [] "e-7173" [] "e-7309" [] "q-40223" []} "basis" 1462 "retractions" [[17592186134187 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S = Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$, where\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set ${a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$ is orthogonal."] [17592186134230 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r = sv + tw$, where $s, t ∈ R$, and\n\n$v = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, w = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w$."] [17592186134198 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S = Span{a_{1}, a_{2}}$ where\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is orthogonal. Round the answer to $2$ decimal places."] [17592186133516 :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal."] [17592186133516 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{a_{1}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{1}}{a_{1} \\cdot a_{1}}a_{1} = (2 \\cdot 1 + 3 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 1)/(1^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2})a_{1} = 1\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nproj_{a_{2}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{2}}{a_{2} \\cdot a_{2}}a_{2} = (2 \\cdot (-2) + 3 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 1)/((-2)^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2})a_{2} =-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= proj_{a_{1}}x + proj_{a_{2}}x \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + (-\\frac{1}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133580 :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the plane $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w}$, where $s,t\\in\\mathbb R$, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the plane given that $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}$."] [17592186133580 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace\n\n$$\nS=\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}\n$$\n\nin $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\nAnd, since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\,\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\,\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{v}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot v}{v \\cdot v}v = (13 \\cdot 2 + (-2) \\cdot 3 + (-1) \\cdot (-1))/(2^{2} + 3^{2} + (-1)^{2})v = \\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nproj_{w}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot w}{w \\cdot w}w = (13 \\cdot 5 + (-2) \\cdot (-3) + (-1) \\cdot 1)/(5^{2} + (-3)^{2} + 1^{2})w = 2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= proj_{v}x + proj_{w}x \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + 2\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133517 :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal."] [17592186133517 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{a_{1}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{1}}{a_{1} \\cdot a_{1}}a_{1} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 1}{1^{2} + 2^{2} + 1^{2}}a_{1} = \\frac{4}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nproj_{a_{2}}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot a_{2}}{a_{2} \\cdot a_{2}}a_{2} = (3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot (-1) + 1 \\cdot 1)/(1^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 1^{2})a_{2} = \\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= proj_{a_{1}}x + proj_{a_{2}}x \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{2}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}(2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} = x^(^)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(x, x^(^)) &= ‖x - x^(^)‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(x_{1} - x^(^)_{1})^{2} + (x_{2} - x^(^)_{2})^{2} + (x_{3} - x^(^)_{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(3 - 2)^{2} + (2 - 2)^{2} + (1 - 2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133582 :question/problem "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf v,\\mathbf w\\}$ where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal."] [17592186133582 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} = \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{v}}\\mathbf{x} + \\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}}\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}\\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{w}\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{v}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot v}{v \\cdot v}v = (\\sqrt{10} \\cdot 0 + 3 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 1)/(0^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 1^{2})v = 2\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nproj_{w}x &= \\frac{x \\cdot w}{w \\cdot w}w = (\\sqrt{10} \\cdot 0 + 3 \\cdot (-1) + 1 \\cdot 1)/(0^{2} + (-1)^{2} + (-1)^{2})w = (-1)\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nproj_{S}x &= proj_{v}x + proj_{w}x \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}$. So, computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{x \\cdot proj_{S}x}{‖x‖‖proj_{S}x‖} \\\\\n&= (\\sqrt{10} \\cdot 0 + 3 \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot 1)/(\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{10})^{2} + 3^{2} + 1^{2}}\\sqrt{0^{2} + 3^{2} + 1^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{20}\\sqrt{10}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{10\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{{\\sqrt2}}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131565 17592186131566 17592186131567 17592186131568 17592186131569 17592186131631 17592186131632 17592186131633 17592186131634 17592186131635 17592186131823 17592186131824 17592186131825 17592186131826 17592186131827] "immutable_fields" [[17592186131564 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131564 :answer-field/id] [17592186131564 :answer-field/key] [17592186131564 :answer-field/type] [17592186131630 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131630 :answer-field/id] [17592186131630 :answer-field/key] [17592186131630 :answer-field/type] [17592186131822 :answer-field/correct] [17592186131822 :answer-field/